xnu 0356: say which process frees blocks the bitmap does not own

`BlockMarkFreeInternal()` prints the range it could not free and marks the volume,
but not who asked. Measured 2026-11-18 on a root that had just been written from
`backup/ios-root-repaired.simg` and verified by `fsck.hfsplus` ("appears to be
OK"), a single boot still produced eight such ranges -- <153186,5>,
<160696,999> three times, <160985,1>, <4050321,1>, <160694,84>, <160778,76> --
so this is not the residue of an earlier dirty reset: something in normal
operation releases extents the allocation bitmap does not own.

The name of the process at the failure is the cheapest way to tell a daemon's
file operation from a kernel thread, and it costs one line, printed for the first
16 failures in a boot. Without it the range says nothing about what to look at.

diff --git a/bsd/mondrian_hfs/core/VolumeAllocation.c b/bsd/mondrian_hfs/core/VolumeAllocation.c
--- a/bsd/mondrian_hfs/core/VolumeAllocation.c
+++ b/bsd/mondrian_hfs/core/VolumeAllocation.c
@@ -157,6 +157,7 @@
 */
 
 
+#include <sys/proc.h>
 #include <sys/types.h>
 #include <sys/buf.h>
 
@@ -3359,6 +3360,22 @@
 #else
 	printf ("hfs: BlockMarkFreeInternal() trying to free unallocated blocks on volume %s <%u, %u>\n",
 			vcb->vcbVN, startingBlock_in, numBlocks_in);
+	/*
+	 * Who asked for it. The range alone does not say which daemon is
+	 * releasing blocks it does not own; the process name does, and it costs
+	 * one line at the failure instead of a flag day of guessing.
+	 */
+	{
+		static int mondrian_free_logged;
+		char who[24];
+
+		if (mondrian_free_logged < 16) {
+			mondrian_free_logged++;
+			proc_selfname(who, (int)sizeof(who));
+			printf("MONDRIAN HFS: free %u+%u asked by %s\n",
+			    startingBlock_in, numBlocks_in, who);
+		}
+	}
 	hfs_mark_inconsistent(vcb, HFS_INCONSISTENCY_DETECTED);
 	err = EIO;
 	goto Exit;
